window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- ARCHAEOLOGY ---------- */
{id:"arc_archaeology",cat:"arc",t:"Archaeology",
 s:"The study of the human past through the things people left behind.",
 l:"Archaeology studies how people lived in the past by looking at what they left behind: tools, pots, bones, buildings, rubbish and even seeds or pollen. It covers times with no writing at all, but also helps with periods we have documents for, because objects often tell a different story than texts. Archaeology is not the same as palaeontology: dinosaurs and other fossils belong to palaeontologists, while archaeologists study humans and our close relatives. Today the work uses laboratories and computers as much as spades.",
 x:"She chose archaeology at university because she wanted to know how ordinary people lived, not just kings.",
 f:"The word comes from the Greek arkhaios, meaning ancient, and logos, meaning study or account.",
 js:"Finding out how people lived long ago by studying what they left behind.",
 jl:"Archaeologists are a bit like detectives of the past. They look at old tools, broken pots, buildings and even ancient rubbish to work out how people lived. Dinosaurs are a different job: people who study them are called palaeontologists.",
 jx:"In the museum, Maya saw a comb made of bone and wondered who had used it thousands of years ago."},
{id:"arc_excavation",cat:"arc",t:"Excavation",
 s:"Careful, step-by-step digging of a site to uncover and record what lies underground.",
 l:"An excavation, often called a dig, removes soil from a site layer by layer, slowly and carefully. Every find is photographed, measured and drawn exactly where it lies, because its position tells as much as the object itself. Digging destroys the very layers it studies, so a site can only be excavated once; good records are what remains afterwards. That is why many sites today are only partly excavated, leaving the rest for future archaeologists with better methods.",
 x:"The excavation of the Roman villa lasted three summers and involved dozens of volunteers.",
 f:"In the eighteen seventies Heinrich Schliemann dug a huge trench through the mound of Troy in Turkey, destroying many layers – a mistake later archaeologists learned from.",
 js:"Digging very slowly and carefully to find things from the past.",
 jl:"At an excavation, archaeologists do not use big diggers near the finds. They scrape away soil a tiny bit at a time with small tools and brushes. Every time they find something, they write down exactly where it was, take photos and make drawings.",
 jx:"On our school trip we watched an excavation, and a student showed us a coin she had just found."},
{id:"arc_stratigraphy",cat:"arc",t:"Stratigraphy",
 s:"The study of layers in the ground and the order in which they formed.",
 l:"Over time, soil, rubbish, collapsed walls and floors pile up on top of each other, forming layers. Stratigraphy reads these layers like pages in a book: as a rule, the deeper a layer is, the older it is, unless something such as a pit or a burrow has disturbed it. By noting which layer each find came from, archaeologists can put objects and events in order even without exact dates. It is one of the most important tools of every excavation.",
 x:"Stratigraphy showed that the stone wall had been built after the wooden house burned down.",
 f:"The basic rule that lower layers are older was set out by the Danish scientist Nicolas Steno in the sixteen sixties, studying rocks rather than ruins.",
 js:"Reading the layers in the ground to find out what is older and what is newer.",
 jl:"Think of a pile of homework on your desk. The sheet at the bottom was put there first, and the one on top last. The ground works in a similar way: deeper layers are usually older. Archaeologists read these layers to put the past in order.",
 jx:"Leo dug a little hole in the sandpit and saw stripes of different sand – just like the layers archaeologists study."},
{id:"arc_artefact",cat:"arc",t:"Artefact",
 s:"Any object made, shaped or used by people.",
 l:"An artefact is anything that humans have made or changed: a stone tool, a bead, a coin, a pot or a sword. Archaeologists separate artefacts from ecofacts, which are natural remains such as seeds, animal bones or pollen that still tell us about human life. An artefact on its own is interesting, but its real value lies in where it was found and what was found with it. In American English the word is spelled artifact.",
 x:"The most common artefacts on the site were broken pieces of cooking pots.",
 f:"The word comes from the Latin phrase arte factum, meaning made with skill.",
 js:"Something that people made or used, found by archaeologists.",
 jl:"An artefact is any object made by people. A stone axe, a bead from a necklace or an old coin are all artefacts. A pencil you lose in the garden could become an artefact for archaeologists far in the future!",
 jx:"Grandpa found a clay pipe in his garden, and the museum said it was an artefact about two hundred years old."},
{id:"arc_radiocarbon",cat:"arc",t:"Radiocarbon dating",
 s:"A method of working out the age of once-living material by measuring a form of carbon in it.",
 l:"All living things take in carbon, including a tiny amount of a radioactive form called carbon fourteen. When a plant or animal dies, it stops taking in new carbon, and the carbon fourteen slowly breaks down at a steady rate: about half of it disappears every five thousand seven hundred years or so. By measuring how much is left, scientists can estimate when the organism died. The method works on wood, bone, charcoal, seeds and cloth, up to about fifty thousand years back, but not on stone or metal.",
 x:"Radiocarbon dating of the charcoal showed that the fire had burned about four thousand years ago.",
 f:"The American chemist Willard Libby developed the method in the late nineteen forties and received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it in nineteen sixty.",
 js:"A science trick that tells how old wood, bones or seeds are.",
 jl:"Living things contain a special kind of carbon. After a plant or animal dies, this carbon slowly disappears, a little at a time, like sand in an hourglass. Scientists measure how much is left and work out how long ago it died.",
 jx:"Thanks to radiocarbon dating, the class learned that the wooden canoe in the museum was older than the pyramids."},
{id:"arc_dendrochronology",cat:"arc",t:"Dendrochronology",
 s:"Dating wood by counting and comparing its yearly growth rings.",
 l:"Trees in many climates grow one ring each year, wide in good years and narrow in cold or dry ones. Because whole regions share the same weather, the pattern of wide and narrow rings is similar in many trees. By matching overlapping patterns from living trees, old beams and buried timbers, scientists have built ring calendars going back thousands of years. A piece of wood can then be dated, sometimes to the exact year the tree was cut down.",
 x:"Dendrochronology showed that the roof beams of the church were cut in the winter of one particular year.",
 f:"The method was developed in the early twentieth century by the American astronomer Andrew Ellicott Douglass, who was looking for links between sunspots and climate.",
 js:"Finding out how old wood is by looking at the rings inside a tree.",
 jl:"If you look at a tree stump, you see circles called rings. A tree usually grows one ring every year. In a good, rainy year the ring is wide, and in a hard year it is thin. Scientists compare these patterns like barcodes to tell exactly when a tree grew.",
 jx:"Zoe counted forty rings on the stump in the park, so the tree was about forty years old."},
{id:"arc_trowel",cat:"arc",t:"Trowel",
 s:"A small, flat, pointed hand tool that archaeologists use to scrape away soil.",
 l:"The trowel is the archaeologist's most familiar tool. It is usually a small pointed builder's trowel with a strong blade, used not to dig deep holes but to scrape the surface thin layer by thin layer, so that changes in the colour and texture of the soil show up. Archaeologists also use brushes, dental picks and sieves for delicate finds, and spades or even machines to remove modern topsoil. Many keep the same trowel for years, and a worn-down blade is a quiet sign of experience.",
 x:"He scraped the floor of the trench with his trowel until the outline of a post hole appeared.",
 f:"The word trowel comes, through French, from a Latin word for a small ladle or scoop.",
 js:"A small flat tool for scraping soil very gently.",
 jl:"A trowel looks like a tiny flat spade with a pointed tip. Archaeologists do not stab the ground with it. They scrape very gently, like spreading butter, so they can see changes in the soil and stop before they damage a find.",
 jx:"Mum lent Kai her garden trowel so he could play archaeologist in the sandpit."},
{id:"arc_hoard",cat:"arc",t:"Hoard",
 s:"A group of valuable objects deliberately hidden or buried together.",
 l:"A hoard is a collection of objects, often coins, jewellery or metal tools, that someone put in the ground on purpose. People hid hoards to keep wealth safe in times of danger, to store metal for recycling, or as offerings to the gods; if the owner never came back, the hoard stayed hidden. Hoards are different from grave goods, which were buried with the dead. Because the objects were put together at one moment, a hoard gives a snapshot of a particular time.",
 x:"A farmer ploughing his field turned up a hoard of silver coins in a broken jar.",
 f:"The Staffordshire Hoard, found by a metal detectorist in a field in England in two thousand and nine, is the largest hoard of Anglo-Saxon gold ever discovered.",
 js:"A pile of treasure that someone hid in the ground long ago.",
 jl:"Long ago there were no banks. When people were afraid of thieves or war, they sometimes buried coins and jewellery to keep them safe. If they never came back, the treasure stayed hidden. Archaeologists call such a find a hoard.",
 jx:"In the story, the children found a hoard of old coins under the roots of a fallen tree."},
{id:"arc_megalith",cat:"arc",t:"Megalith",
 s:"A huge stone, or a structure of huge stones, set up in prehistoric times.",
 l:"Megaliths are large stones raised by people, mostly between about seven thousand and four thousand years ago. They include single standing stones, called menhirs, stone circles, rows of stones and dolmens, which are tombs made of upright stones with a flat stone on top. Builders moved stones weighing many tonnes using ropes, wooden sledges, levers and lots of people working together. Many megaliths were linked to burials, gatherings or the movements of the Sun.",
 x:"On holiday in Brittany we walked past long lines of megaliths standing in the fields.",
 f:"Near Carnac in Brittany, France, more than three thousand prehistoric standing stones are arranged in long rows.",
 js:"A giant stone that people set up a very long time ago.",
 jl:"Thousands of years ago, people moved enormous stones, heavier than cars, without any machines. They used ropes, logs and many helpers. Some stones stand alone, some make circles. These giant stones are called megaliths, which means big stones.",
 jx:"Ben stood next to the megalith and it was three times taller than him."},
{id:"arc_barrow",cat:"arc",t:"Barrow",
 s:"An ancient mound of earth or stones built over one or more graves.",
 l:"A barrow, also called a burial mound or tumulus, is a heap of earth or stones raised over a burial. Long barrows were built in Britain in the Neolithic period, while round barrows are mostly from the Bronze Age. Some covered stone chambers used for many burials over generations; others covered a single important person with grave goods. Barrows can still be seen as bumps in fields and on hilltops, and many are now protected monuments.",
 x:"The footpath led along a ridge dotted with round barrows.",
 f:"The word barrow comes from the Old English beorg, meaning a hill or mound.",
 js:"A big hill of earth that people built long ago over a special grave.",
 jl:"Long ago, when an important person died, people sometimes built a hill of earth on top of their resting place, so everyone would remember them. These hills are called barrows. You can still see some of them as round bumps in the countryside.",
 jx:"Dad pointed at a round bump on the hill and said it was a barrow over four thousand years old."},
{id:"arc_hillfort",cat:"arc",t:"Hillfort",
 s:"A settlement on a hill protected by banks, ditches or walls.",
 l:"A hillfort is a place on high ground surrounded by earth ramparts, ditches and often wooden fences or stone walls. Many in Europe date from the Bronze and Iron Ages. Some were permanent villages, others were places to gather, trade or shelter in danger. Their banks can still be traced in the landscape today. In Slavic lands similar fortified sites, called grody, were built in the early Middle Ages, and many later towns grew next to them.",
 x:"From the top of the hillfort you can see why people chose it: no one could approach unseen.",
 f:"Maiden Castle in Dorset, with its huge rings of earth banks, is one of the largest Iron Age hillforts in Europe.",
 js:"A village on a hill with walls and ditches around it to keep people safe.",
 jl:"Long ago, people liked to live on hills because they could see enemies coming. They dug deep ditches and built high banks of earth and wooden fences all around. These places are called hillforts, and you can still walk on their banks today.",
 jx:"We rolled down the grassy banks of the old hillfort and pretended to be guards."},
{id:"arc_mummy",cat:"arc",t:"Mummy",
 s:"A body preserved from decay, either on purpose or by natural conditions.",
 l:"A mummy is a body whose skin and soft tissues have survived, because something stopped decay. Ancient Egyptians made mummies on purpose: they removed organs, dried the body with a salt called natron and wrapped it in linen, believing the body was needed in the afterlife. Natural mummies form in very dry deserts, freezing ice or waterlogged bogs. Today scientists study mummies with scanners instead of unwrapping them, learning about diet, health and age without causing damage.",
 x:"The museum's scans showed that the Egyptian mummy had been a young man with healthy teeth.",
 f:"The word mummy comes, through Latin, from the Arabic mūmiyā, a name for bitumen, because the dark coating on some mummies was thought to be tar.",
 js:"A body from long ago that was kept safe from rotting, like in ancient Egypt.",
 jl:"In ancient Egypt, people believed the body was needed in the next life. So they dried it carefully with special salt and wrapped it in many layers of cloth. That is a mummy. Today scientists can look inside mummies with scanners, without unwrapping them.",
 jx:"At the museum, Amira learned that even cats were sometimes made into mummies in ancient Egypt."},
{id:"arc_hieroglyphs",cat:"arc",t:"Hieroglyphs",
 s:"The picture-based writing system of ancient Egypt.",
 l:"Egyptian hieroglyphs are signs that look like birds, eyes, reeds or people, but they are not just pictures. Some stand for sounds, some for whole words, and some help show the meaning of the word next to them. They were carved on temples and tombs for more than three thousand years, while scribes used quicker, simpler scripts for everyday writing. After ancient Egyptian religion faded, the knowledge of how to read them was lost for about fourteen centuries.",
 x:"The guide translated the hieroglyphs above the doorway: they gave the name of the king.",
 f:"The name comes from Greek words meaning sacred carvings.",
 js:"Picture signs that ancient Egyptians used for writing.",
 jl:"Ancient Egyptians wrote with little pictures called hieroglyphs. An owl, a hand or a snake could each stand for a sound, a bit like our letters. Some signs stood for whole words. They carved them on the walls of temples and tombs.",
 jx:"Noah wrote his name in hieroglyphs using a chart from his library book."},
{id:"arc_rosetta",cat:"arc",t:"Rosetta Stone",
 s:"An ancient Egyptian stone slab with the same text in three scripts, which helped decipher hieroglyphs.",
 l:"The Rosetta Stone is a broken slab of dark stone carved in one hundred and ninety-six BC with a decree of priests honouring King Ptolemy the Fifth. The same text appears in three scripts: hieroglyphs, the everyday Egyptian script called Demotic, and ancient Greek. Because scholars could read the Greek, they could compare it with the Egyptian versions. It was found in seventeen ninety-nine near the town of Rashid, called Rosetta in Europe, and is now in the British Museum.",
 x:"Learning a new language with a bilingual book felt like having my own Rosetta Stone.",
 f:"The French scholar Jean-François Champollion announced in eighteen twenty-two that he had cracked how hieroglyphs work, using the stone as a key.",
 js:"A famous stone that helped people learn to read hieroglyphs again.",
 jl:"For a very long time, nobody could read Egyptian hieroglyphs. Then a stone was found with the same message written three ways, and one of them was Greek, which people could still read. By comparing them like a puzzle, a clever scholar finally worked out the hieroglyphs.",
 jx:"In class we made our own Rosetta Stone, writing one sentence in English, in code and in pictures."},
{id:"arc_sherd",cat:"arc",t:"Sherd (potsherd)",
 s:"A broken piece of pottery found at an archaeological site.",
 l:"Sherds are the most common finds on many sites, because fired clay breaks easily but almost never rots. Pottery styles, shapes and decorations changed over time and from place to place, so experts can often date a layer or tell where a pot was made from a few fragments. Traces of food left inside can even be analysed to show what people cooked. Sherds are counted, weighed and sometimes glued back together into whole vessels.",
 x:"The volunteers spent the rainy afternoon washing and labelling hundreds of sherds.",
 f:"In ancient Athens citizens voted to banish a politician by scratching his name on potsherds called ostraka, which gave us the word ostracism.",
 js:"A broken bit of an old pot.",
 jl:"Pots break easily, but the pieces last for thousands of years in the ground. That is why archaeologists find so many broken bits, called sherds. The colour, shape and patterns help them tell how old a pot is and where it was made.",
 jx:"Ella found a sherd with a blue pattern and the archaeologist said it came from an old plate."},
{id:"arc_pompeii",cat:"arc",t:"Pompeii",
 s:"A Roman town in southern Italy buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in seventy-nine AD.",
 l:"Pompeii was a busy Roman town near Naples until Mount Vesuvius erupted in seventy-nine AD and covered it with metres of ash and pumice. The town was sealed, and with it shops, bakeries, painted houses, graffiti on walls and food on tables. Excavations began in the seventeen forties and continue today. Because so much ordinary life was preserved at one moment, Pompeii shows daily life in a Roman town better than almost any other place.",
 x:"In Pompeii you can still see the ruts that cart wheels wore into the stone streets.",
 f:"An eyewitness account of the eruption survives in two letters by Pliny the Younger, who watched it from across the bay.",
 js:"A Roman town that was covered by ash from a volcano long ago.",
 jl:"About two thousand years ago there was a busy Roman town called Pompeii. A nearby volcano erupted and covered it with ash. Because it was covered so quickly, the town stayed almost like a time capsule, with shops, ovens and paintings. Today visitors can walk its old streets.",
 jx:"In Pompeii, Tom saw a picture of a dog on a doorstep with the Latin words for beware of the dog."},
{id:"arc_lidar",cat:"arc",t:"Lidar",
 s:"A laser scanning method, often used from the air, that maps the ground in fine detail, even under trees.",
 l:"Lidar sends out very many laser pulses, usually from a plane or drone, and measures how long each one takes to bounce back. Some pulses slip through gaps in the leaves and reach the ground, so computers can remove the trees and show the bare landscape underneath. Old field systems, roads, burial mounds and ruins hidden in forests suddenly appear. Lidar has changed archaeology in places where thick vegetation made walking surveys almost impossible.",
 x:"Lidar revealed an ancient road running straight through the woods behind the village.",
 f:"Lidar surveys in the forests of northern Guatemala have revealed tens of thousands of previously unknown Maya structures.",
 js:"Using lasers from a plane to see old ruins hidden under trees.",
 jl:"Imagine shining millions of tiny laser beams from a plane. Some of them sneak between the leaves and touch the ground. A computer uses them to draw a map with the trees taken away. Suddenly, hidden roads and old walls appear!",
 jx:"The lidar map showed the outline of a lost castle in the forest near our school."},
{id:"arc_underwater",cat:"arc",t:"Underwater archaeology",
 s:"The study of shipwrecks, sunken settlements and other remains under water.",
 l:"Underwater archaeologists dive to shipwrecks, flooded towns and harbours, and remains in lakes and rivers. They work with the same care as on land, measuring and recording everything, but with limited air, cold water and poor visibility. Water without much oxygen can preserve wood, rope, leather and food remarkably well. Raised objects must be conserved quickly, because waterlogged wood can crack and shrink as soon as it dries.",
 x:"Underwater archaeology showed how sailors stored their food on a medieval trading ship.",
 f:"The Mary Rose, a warship of King Henry the Eighth that sank in fifteen forty-five, was raised from the seabed in nineteen eighty-two and can be seen in Portsmouth.",
 js:"Archaeology under water, exploring old shipwrecks.",
 jl:"Some archaeologists put on diving suits and explore old shipwrecks at the bottom of the sea or lakes. Cold water can keep wood and rope safe for hundreds of years. Divers measure and photograph everything before anything is brought up.",
 jx:"Ollie dreams of becoming an underwater archaeologist and finding a pirate ship."},
{id:"arc_experimental",cat:"arc",t:"Experimental archaeology",
 s:"Testing ideas about the past by recreating ancient tools, buildings or techniques.",
 l:"Experimental archaeology asks practical questions: how long does it take to cut down a tree with a stone axe, how hot can a clay oven get, how would this house stand up to winter? To find out, researchers make copies of ancient objects and buildings using old methods, then use them and record the results. This can confirm or rule out explanations. Reconstructed villages and open-air museums often grow out of such experiments.",
 x:"In an experimental archaeology project, students tried to make bronze in a clay furnace.",
 f:"In nineteen forty-seven Thor Heyerdahl sailed a balsa-wood raft, the Kon-Tiki, from Peru to Polynesia to test whether such voyages were possible.",
 js:"Trying out old ways of making things to learn how people lived.",
 jl:"How did people bake bread without an oven? How did they cut wood with a stone axe? Some archaeologists find out by trying it themselves! They build old-style houses, make pots and tools, and see what works.",
 jx:"At the museum workshop, Ana made fire with a flint and a piece of iron, like people long ago."},
{id:"arc_neolithic",cat:"arc",t:"Neolithic",
 s:"The New Stone Age, when people began farming and living in permanent villages.",
 l:"The Neolithic is the period when people started to grow crops and keep animals instead of only hunting and gathering. It began more than ten thousand years ago in the Middle East and spread gradually to Europe and other regions, at different times in different places. People built permanent houses, made pottery, ground stone tools and later raised many megaliths. The change to farming, often called the Neolithic Revolution, transformed diets, landscapes and societies.",
 x:"The Neolithic village had houses with stone walls, hearths and shelf-like beds.",
 f:"The name comes from Greek words meaning new stone, because people first noticed this period from its polished stone tools.",
 js:"The time in the Stone Age when people started farming.",
 jl:"For a very long time, people moved around, hunting animals and picking berries. Then, in the New Stone Age, called the Neolithic, they learned to grow wheat and keep sheep and goats. They built houses and stayed in one place. Villages were born!",
 jx:"In history, we learned that Neolithic people made pots to store their grain."},
{id:"arc_terracotta",cat:"arc",t:"Terracotta Army",
 s:"Thousands of life-size clay soldiers buried near the tomb of China's first emperor.",
 l:"The Terracotta Army was made for Qin Shi Huang, who united China in two hundred and twenty-one BC and became its first emperor. Thousands of life-size soldiers, horses and chariots of fired clay were placed in pits near his tomb close to the city of Xi'an, to guard him in the afterlife. The figures have individual hairstyles, clothes and expressions. Excavation is still going on, and the emperor's burial mound itself has not been opened.",
 x:"Standing above the pit of the Terracotta Army, we saw rows and rows of clay soldiers.",
 f:"The army was discovered in nineteen seventy-four by farmers digging a well, and the figures were originally painted in bright colours that have mostly faded.",
 js:"A huge army of clay soldiers made for a Chinese emperor.",
 jl:"More than two thousand years ago, the first emperor of China wanted guards even after his life ended. So workers made thousands of life-size soldiers and horses out of clay. They were buried near his tomb and found by farmers digging a well.",
 jx:"Lily made a small clay soldier in art class, inspired by the Terracotta Army."},
{id:"arc_cave_art",cat:"arc",t:"Cave art",
 s:"Paintings, drawings and carvings made by prehistoric people on cave walls.",
 l:"Cave art includes images of animals such as horses, bison and deer, hand stencils and abstract signs, made tens of thousands of years ago. Artists used charcoal and coloured earth like red and yellow ochre, sometimes blowing paint around their hands. Some of the most famous caves are Lascaux in France and Altamira in Spain. Many caves are now closed or strictly limited to visitors, because breath, warmth and light can damage the paintings, so exact copies have been built nearby.",
 x:"The cave art showed a herd of horses that seemed to move in the flickering torchlight.",
 f:"The paintings at Altamira were first noticed in eighteen seventy-nine by a young girl, María, who looked up at the ceiling while her father searched the cave floor.",
 js:"Pictures that people painted on cave walls a very long time ago.",
 jl:"Thousands and thousands of years ago, people painted animals on the walls of caves. They used charcoal and coloured earth. Some even blew paint around their hands to leave a handprint. These pictures are still there today!",
 jx:"After learning about cave art, Sam drew a bison with charcoal on brown paper."},
{id:"arc_in_situ",cat:"arc",t:"In situ",
 s:"Describing a find still in the exact place where it was left in the past.",
 l:"In situ is Latin for in place. When archaeologists say an object was found in situ, they mean nobody has moved it since it was buried, so its position and surroundings can be trusted as evidence. A pot found in situ on a house floor tells us about that house; the same pot found in a ploughed field could have come from anywhere. Sometimes important remains, such as mosaics, are deliberately left in situ and protected rather than lifted.",
 x:"The skeleton of the horse was found in situ, lying exactly where it had been buried.",
 f:"Besides archaeology, the phrase is used in medicine, biology and conservation for anything studied or kept in its original place.",
 js:"Still in its original place, not moved.",
 jl:"In situ is a Latin phrase that means in its place. If an archaeologist finds a cup exactly where someone left it long ago, it is in situ. That is important, because it shows how the cup was used.",
 jx:"Our teacher said the lunchbox under my bed was in situ – nobody had moved it for a week!"},
{id:"arc_conservation",cat:"arc",t:"Conservation (of finds)",
 s:"Treating and protecting finds so they survive for the future.",
 l:"Once an object leaves the ground, its conditions change suddenly: metal can corrode, wood can crack, colours can fade. Conservators clean finds gently, stabilise them with suitable materials and store them at the right temperature and humidity. A key principle is to do only what is needed and, where possible, use treatments that can be reversed later. Conservation is different from restoration, which tries to make an object look complete again.",
 x:"The rusted sword went straight to the conservation laboratory.",
 f:"The waterlogged timbers of the Mary Rose were sprayed for years with a waxy chemical, polyethylene glycol, and then slowly dried so they would not shrink and crack.",
 js:"Taking care of old finds so they do not fall apart.",
 jl:"When old things come out of the ground, they can be very fragile. Iron might start to rust and wood might crack. Special experts called conservators clean them gently and keep them safe in the right conditions, so people can see them for many years.",
 jx:"The conservator used a tiny soft brush to clean the old coin."},
{id:"arc_ancient_dna",cat:"arc",t:"Ancient DNA",
 s:"Genetic material recovered from old bones, teeth or other remains.",
 l:"Ancient DNA is DNA extracted from remains that can be thousands of years old. It is usually broken into tiny fragments and easily mixed with modern contamination, so labs work in very clean rooms. Studying it shows how populations moved and mixed, what diseases people carried and how plants and animals were domesticated. It can also show family links between people buried in the same cemetery.",
 x:"Ancient DNA showed that the two people in the grave were mother and son.",
 f:"In twenty twenty-two the Swedish geneticist Svante Pääbo received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for sequencing the genome of Neanderthals.",
 js:"Tiny instructions inside old bones that tell scientists about people long ago.",
 jl:"Every living thing carries tiny instructions called DNA. Scientists can sometimes find bits of DNA inside very old bones and teeth. It helps them learn who people were related to and where their families came from.",
 jx:"The documentary showed scientists in white suits studying ancient DNA from a tooth."},
{id:"arc_geophysics",cat:"arc",t:"Geophysical survey",
 s:"Exploring what lies underground with instruments, without digging.",
 l:"Geophysical survey uses instruments carried over a field to detect buried features. A magnetometer picks up tiny changes in magnetism caused by ditches, pits, hearths and kilns. Ground-penetrating radar sends radio waves into the ground and records echoes from walls and floors. Resistivity measures how easily electricity passes through soil. The results appear as maps that help archaeologists decide where, or whether, to dig.",
 x:"Before any digging, a geophysical survey revealed the outline of a Roman building.",
 f:"Heating changes the magnetism of clay, so ancient hearths, ovens and kilns often show up especially clearly on magnetometer maps.",
 js:"Using special machines to see what is underground without digging.",
 jl:"Archaeologists can walk across a field with special machines that act a bit like X-ray glasses for the ground. They show hidden walls, ditches and old fireplaces. That way, archaeologists know where to dig, or can leave the place safely untouched.",
 jx:"The survey map looked blurry to Mia, but the archaeologist showed her the shape of a hidden house."},
{id:"arc_en_stonehenge",cat:"arc",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Stonehenge",
 s:"A prehistoric stone monument on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, England.",
 l:"Stonehenge was built and rebuilt in several stages over many centuries, starting around five thousand years ago with a circular ditch and bank. Later came the huge sarsen stones, topped with horizontal lintels, and smaller bluestones brought from the Preseli Hills in Wales, over two hundred kilometres away. The monument is aligned with the sunrise at the summer solstice and the sunset at the winter solstice. It is part of a wider landscape of barrows and other monuments and is a World Heritage Site.",
 x:"Thousands of people gather at Stonehenge to watch the sunrise on the longest day of the year.",
 f:"The smaller bluestones were brought from west Wales, and how people moved them so far is still debated.",
 js:"A famous circle of giant stones in England, built thousands of years ago.",
 jl:"Stonehenge is a ring of enormous stones in a field in England. People built it around five thousand years ago, without any machines. Some stones came all the way from Wales! On the longest day of the year, the Sun rises in line with the stones.",
 jx:"On our trip to Stonehenge, Grandma told us people had been coming to see the stones for centuries."},
{id:"arc_en_sutton_hoo",cat:"arc",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Sutton Hoo",
 s:"An Anglo-Saxon burial ground in Suffolk, famous for a ship burial uncovered in 1939.",
 l:"At Sutton Hoo, near Woodbridge in Suffolk, a group of burial mounds sits above the River Deben. In nineteen thirty-nine the landowner Edith Pretty asked a local self-taught archaeologist, Basil Brown, to dig the largest mound. He found the imprint of a wooden ship about twenty-seven metres long, its timbers rotted away, with a burial chamber full of treasures: a helmet, gold buckles, silver bowls and weapons. It is often thought to be the grave of Rædwald, king of East Anglia, who died around the year six hundred and twenty-five.",
 x:"The Sutton Hoo helmet in the British Museum has become a symbol of Anglo-Saxon England.",
 f:"Edith Pretty gave the Sutton Hoo treasure to the nation, and the story of the dig was told in the film The Dig in twenty twenty-one.",
 js:"A place in England where a king was buried in a big wooden ship full of treasure.",
 jl:"About fourteen hundred years ago, an important person, probably a king, was buried inside a whole ship under a big mound of earth in England. In nineteen thirty-nine, a digger called Basil Brown found it. The wood had rotted, but the shape of the ship and amazing treasures were left.",
 jx:"Harry drew the Sutton Hoo helmet for his school project on the Anglo-Saxons."},
{id:"arc_en_vindolanda",cat:"arc",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Vindolanda tablets",
 s:"Thin wooden writing tablets from a Roman fort near Hadrian's Wall in northern England.",
 l:"Vindolanda was a Roman fort just south of Hadrian's Wall. Since nineteen seventy-three, excavations there have found hundreds of thin wooden leaf tablets with ink writing, preserved in wet, oxygen-poor ground. They date from around the end of the first century and the start of the second century AD. They include army reports, lists of supplies, requests for leave and private letters, giving a rare, personal view of life on the edge of the Roman Empire.",
 x:"One Vindolanda tablet mentions socks and underpants sent to a soldier to keep him warm.",
 f:"Among them is a birthday party invitation from Claudia Severa to her friend Sulpicia Lepidina, one of the earliest known pieces of Latin handwriting by a woman.",
 js:"Very old Roman letters written on thin wood, found in England.",
 jl:"Near Hadrian's Wall, archaeologists found letters that Roman soldiers and their families wrote almost two thousand years ago, on thin pieces of wood. One is an invitation to a birthday party! Others ask for warm socks.",
 jx:"Our class wrote party invitations in the style of the Vindolanda tablets."},
{id:"arc_en_treasure",cat:"arc",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Treasure Act",
 s:"The law that requires finds of treasure in England, Wales and Northern Ireland to be reported.",
 l:"Under the Treasure Act of nineteen ninety-six, anyone who finds objects that count as treasure must report them to the local coroner within fourteen days. Treasure includes, among other things, most finds of gold or silver objects over three hundred years old and groups of old coins found together. A museum may then acquire the find, and a reward is usually shared between the finder and the landowner. Other finds can be recorded voluntarily with the Portable Antiquities Scheme, which helps build a map of the past.",
 x:"The detectorist reported the gold ring under the Treasure Act, and a local museum bought it.",
 f:"The Portable Antiquities Scheme has recorded well over a million finds made by members of the public.",
 js:"A rule that says if you find real old treasure, you must tell the right people.",
 jl:"If someone in England finds very old gold, silver or a group of old coins, they must not just keep it. They have to tell an official called a coroner. Then a museum can look after it so everyone can see it, and the finder usually gets a reward.",
 jx:"When Jack's uncle found an old silver coin, he reported it, just as the Treasure Act says."},
/* ---------- MEDICINE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"m_herd_immunity",cat:"med",t:"Herd immunity",
 s:"Protection of a whole community when enough people are immune to an infection.",
 l:"When a large share of people are immune to a disease, through vaccination or past infection, the germ struggles to find new people to infect and its spread slows or stops. This also protects those who cannot be vaccinated, such as very young babies or people with weak immune systems. The share needed depends on how contagious the disease is: the more easily it spreads, the higher the share. If vaccination rates fall, outbreaks can return.",
 x:"High vaccination rates in the school helped keep herd immunity against measles.",
 f:"Measles is so contagious that about ninety-five out of every hundred people need to be immune to stop it spreading.",
 js:"When so many people are protected that a germ cannot spread.",
 jl:"Imagine a game of tag where most players are safe and cannot be tagged. The chaser soon gets stuck. Germs are similar: when most people are protected, for example by vaccines, a germ cannot jump from person to person. This also keeps safe the people who cannot have vaccines.",
 jx:"The nurse explained that by getting vaccinated, Leo also helps protect his baby sister."},
{id:"m_diagnosis_prognosis",cat:"med",t:"Diagnosis and prognosis",
 s:"Diagnosis names what is wrong; prognosis is the expected course and outcome.",
 l:"A diagnosis is the conclusion about what condition a person has, based on symptoms, examination and tests. A prognosis is the doctor's estimate of how the condition is likely to develop: how long recovery may take or how likely it is to come back. A prognosis is always a probability, not a promise, because people respond differently. Asking about both helps patients understand their situation and make decisions about treatment.",
 x:"The diagnosis was a sprained ankle, and the prognosis was full recovery in a few weeks.",
 f:"Both words come from Greek: diagnosis means telling apart, and prognosis means knowing beforehand.",
 js:"Diagnosis is what is wrong; prognosis is how it will probably go.",
 jl:"When you feel ill, the doctor works out what is wrong. That is the diagnosis, for example a cold. Then the doctor might say how long it will take to get better. That guess about the future is called the prognosis.",
 jx:"The doctor's diagnosis was a cold, and her prognosis was that Mia would feel better by the weekend."},
{id:"m_anaesthesia",cat:"med",t:"Anaesthesia",
 s:"Medicines and techniques that stop a patient feeling pain during treatment.",
 l:"Anaesthesia prevents pain during operations and procedures. Local anaesthesia numbs one small area, as at the dentist, while the patient stays awake. Regional anaesthesia numbs a larger part of the body, such as the lower half. General anaesthesia puts the patient into a controlled, deep unconsciousness, carefully monitored by an anaesthetist. Before anaesthesia, surgery had to be done as fast as possible while patients were awake, which made many operations impossible.",
 x:"The dentist gave a local anaesthetic, so I felt nothing while the tooth was filled.",
 f:"In eighteen forty-six, at Massachusetts General Hospital in Boston, William Morton gave a public demonstration of ether anaesthesia during an operation.",
 js:"Medicine that stops you feeling pain during an operation or at the dentist.",
 jl:"Sometimes doctors need to fix something inside the body, or the dentist needs to fix a tooth. Anaesthesia stops it from hurting. Sometimes only one small spot goes numb. For bigger operations, you go into a deep, safe sleep and wake up when it is finished.",
 jx:"After the anaesthetic, Sam's cheek felt funny and puffy, but nothing hurt."},
{id:"m_epidemic_pandemic",cat:"med",t:"Epidemic and pandemic",
 s:"An epidemic is an unusual rise of a disease in one area; a pandemic spreads across many countries and continents.",
 l:"An epidemic is when a disease affects many more people than usual in a particular region or group. A pandemic is an epidemic that spreads across several countries or continents, affecting very large numbers of people. A disease that is always present at a steady level in a region is called endemic. These words describe how widely an illness spreads, not how serious it is for each person.",
 x:"The flu epidemic closed several schools in the region, but it never became a pandemic.",
 f:"The World Health Organization described COVID-19 as a pandemic on the eleventh of March, twenty twenty.",
 js:"An epidemic is when lots of people get ill in one place; a pandemic is all over the world.",
 jl:"Sometimes an illness spreads to many people in one town or country. That is an epidemic. If it spreads to lots of countries around the world, it is a pandemic. The word pan means all, like in pan-American.",
 jx:"Grandpa said that during the pandemic he learned to video-call the whole family."},
{id:"m_fever",cat:"med",t:"Fever",
 s:"A body temperature higher than normal, usually a sign that the body is fighting an infection.",
 l:"A fever happens when the brain raises the body's temperature setting, often in response to an infection. The higher temperature helps the immune system work and makes life harder for some germs. People with a fever may feel cold and shiver while their temperature is rising, then hot and sweaty later. Normal temperature varies slightly between people and through the day. It is important to drink enough and to seek medical advice for very high fevers, fevers in young babies or fevers with worrying symptoms.",
 x:"He had a fever of thirty-eight and a half degrees, so he stayed home from work.",
 f:"In the nineteenth century the German doctor Carl Wunderlich made the figure of thirty-seven degrees Celsius famous as normal body temperature, after measuring many thousands of patients.",
 js:"When your body gets hotter than usual because it is fighting germs.",
 jl:"When germs get into your body, your body may turn up its own heating to fight them. That is a fever. You might feel hot, tired or shivery. Resting, drinking water and telling a grown-up helps. A doctor can check if it is serious.",
 jx:"Ella had a fever, so Dad brought her water and her favourite blanket."},
{id:"m_en_nhs111",cat:"med",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"NHS 111",
 s:"A free phone and online service for urgent medical help that is not a life-threatening emergency.",
 l:"NHS 111 helps people who need medical help quickly but are not in a life-threatening situation. You can phone one, one, one or use the website, answer questions about your symptoms, and be told where to go: a pharmacy, a GP, an urgent treatment centre, or A and E. It works day and night, every day of the year. For emergencies such as chest pain, severe bleeding or someone who has collapsed, you should call nine, nine, nine instead.",
 x:"Her son's rash was getting worse late at night, so she called NHS 111 for advice.",
 f:"In Scotland the one, one, one number connects to the service called NHS 24.",
 js:"A number to call in the UK when you need a doctor's advice quickly but it is not an emergency.",
 jl:"In the UK, if someone feels unwell and is not sure what to do, a grown-up can call one, one, one. A nurse or adviser asks questions and tells them where to go. If it is a real emergency, like someone who will not wake up, you call nine, nine, nine.",
 jx:"When Ruby's temperature went up at night, Mum called NHS 111 to ask what to do."},
/* ---------- LAW (+6) ---------- */
{id:"l_copyright",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Copyright",
 s:"The legal right of the creator of an original work to control how it is copied and used.",
 l:"Copyright protects original works such as books, music, photographs, films, drawings and computer code. In the UK it arises automatically when the work is created; you do not need to register it. The owner decides who may copy, publish, perform or adapt the work, often by giving licences. For most works, copyright lasts for the creator's lifetime plus seventy years. Copyright protects the expression, not the idea itself, and there are limited exceptions, for example for criticism, quotation or private study.",
 x:"The band asked for permission before using the song, because it was still under copyright.",
 f:"The Statute of Anne of seventeen ten is often called the world's first copyright law.",
 js:"A rule that says the person who made a song, story or picture decides how others can use it.",
 jl:"When you write a story or draw a picture, it belongs to you. Others should ask before copying it or selling it. That is called copyright. Authors, musicians and artists earn money because of it.",
 jx:"Our teacher reminded us to name the artist when we used a picture from the internet."},
{id:"l_mediation",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Mediation",
 s:"A process where a neutral person helps people in a dispute reach their own agreement.",
 l:"In mediation, a trained, impartial mediator helps both sides talk, understand each other's concerns and look for a solution. The mediator does not judge or decide; the parties agree their own outcome. It is usually quicker, cheaper and less stressful than going to court and is often used for family, neighbour, workplace and business disputes. Before applying to a family court in England and Wales, people are usually expected to attend an information meeting about mediation.",
 x:"Instead of suing each other, the two neighbours tried mediation and agreed on a new fence.",
 f:"The meeting expected before many family court applications is known by the short name MIAM, Mediation Information and Assessment Meeting.",
 js:"When a calm helper helps two sides solve an argument together.",
 jl:"Sometimes people argue and cannot agree. A mediator is a calm helper who does not take sides. They help both people listen and find an answer they are both happy with. Some schools have pupils trained as peer mediators.",
 jx:"Two friends arguing over the football used the playground mediator and agreed to take turns."},
{id:"l_witness",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Witness",
 s:"A person who gives evidence in court about what they saw, heard or know.",
 l:"A witness tells the court what they know about the facts of a case. Before giving evidence, witnesses swear an oath or make a solemn affirmation, a non-religious promise, to tell the truth. They are questioned by the side that called them and can then be cross-examined by the other side. Lying under oath is the crime of perjury. Courts can order a witness to attend, and vulnerable witnesses may give evidence behind a screen or by video link.",
 x:"The witness described the colour of the car she saw driving away.",
 f:"Expert witnesses, such as doctors or engineers, are allowed to give opinions in their field, while ordinary witnesses should describe facts they observed.",
 js:"A person who tells the court what they saw or heard.",
 jl:"A witness is someone who saw or heard something important. In court, they promise to tell the truth and then answer questions. Their words help the judge or jury understand what really happened.",
 jx:"Ava was a witness when the vase fell – she saw the cat knock it over!"},
{id:"l_burden_proof",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Burden of proof",
 s:"The duty of a party to prove what they claim in court.",
 l:"The burden of proof decides who has to prove something and to what level. In criminal cases the prosecution must prove guilt, and the defendant does not have to prove innocence. The jury must be sure of guilt, often described as beyond reasonable doubt. In civil cases the person bringing the claim usually has the burden, but the level is lower: the balance of probabilities, meaning more likely than not. Who bears this burden can decide the case when the evidence is unclear.",
 x:"The judge reminded the jury that the burden of proof lay on the prosecution.",
 f:"An old Latin legal saying expresses the idea: the burden of proof lies on the one who asserts, not on the one who denies.",
 js:"The rule about who has to show that their story is true.",
 jl:"If someone says you took their pen, they have to show it is true. You do not have to prove you did not. That is the idea of the burden of proof: the person who makes a claim must back it up.",
 jx:"Ben said Sam ate his crisps, so Ben had to show some proof – crumbs did not count!"},
{id:"l_lawsuit",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Claim (lawsuit)",
 s:"A case brought to a civil court by one person or organisation against another.",
 l:"In a civil claim, one party asks a court to settle a dispute, for example about unpaid money, a faulty product or damage to property. In England and Wales the person bringing the case is called the claimant and the other side is the defendant. Before going to court, parties are expected to exchange information and try to settle. A claim is started by filing a claim form, and most claims are settled or decided without a full trial.",
 x:"After months of unpaid invoices, the company brought a claim against its client.",
 f:"Since reforms in nineteen ninety-nine, the old word plaintiff was replaced by claimant in the civil courts of England and Wales.",
 js:"When someone asks a court to solve a problem with someone else.",
 jl:"Sometimes people cannot agree, for example about money that has not been paid back. One of them can ask a court to decide. That is a claim. The person who asks is called the claimant.",
 jx:"In the news, a shop brought a claim because a builder never finished its new roof."},
{id:"l_en_royal_assent",cat:"law",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Royal Assent",
 s:"The monarch's formal agreement that turns a bill passed by Parliament into an Act of Parliament.",
 l:"In the United Kingdom, a proposed law, called a bill, must pass through the House of Commons and usually the House of Lords. The final step is Royal Assent, when the monarch formally agrees, after which the bill becomes an Act and law. Today this is a formality: by long-standing constitutional convention the monarch always agrees to bills passed by Parliament. The assent is announced in Parliament using a traditional Norman French phrase.",
 x:"The new law received Royal Assent on Thursday and came into force a month later.",
 f:"The last time a monarch refused Royal Assent was in seventeen hundred and eight, when Queen Anne withheld it from the Scottish Militia Bill.",
 js:"When the King or Queen formally says yes to a new law.",
 jl:"In the UK, new laws are discussed and voted on in Parliament. At the very end, the King or Queen gives a formal yes, called Royal Assent. Today they always say yes, but it is an old tradition that is still part of making laws.",
 jx:"Our class learned that a bill becomes an Act after Royal Assent."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"g_hyperbole",cat:"gen",t:"Hyperbole",
 s:"Deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis or effect, not meant literally.",
 l:"Hyperbole is exaggeration that no one is expected to take literally. When someone says they have told you a million times, or that a bag weighs a tonne, they are using hyperbole to show strong feelings. It is common in everyday speech, advertising, comedy and poetry. Its opposite is understatement, saying less than you mean. For some listeners, including many autistic people, it can help if the speaker makes clear when they are exaggerating.",
 x:"“I'm so hungry I could eat a horse” is a classic example of hyperbole.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek and literally means throwing beyond.",
 js:"Saying something much bigger than it really is, to make a point.",
 jl:"If you say “I have a million things to do,” you do not really have a million things. You are exaggerating to show you are very busy. That is hyperbole. People use it a lot when they are excited, tired or funny.",
 jx:"“This backpack weighs a ton!” said Max – but it was only full of books."},
{id:"g_onomatopoeia",cat:"gen",t:"Onomatopoeia",
 s:"A word that imitates the sound it describes.",
 l:"Onomatopoeia means words like buzz, splash, sizzle, tick-tock or cuckoo, which sound like what they describe. They make writing lively and are popular in comics, poems and children's books. Interestingly, languages imitate the same sounds differently: animals do not change, but the words people use for their noises do. Some onomatopoeic words have become names, such as the cuckoo, named after its call.",
 x:"The comic strip was full of onomatopoeia: bang, whoosh and crash.",
 f:"An English dog says woof, a Polish dog says hau hau, and a Japanese dog says wan wan.",
 js:"A word that sounds like the noise it means, like buzz or splash.",
 jl:"Some words copy sounds. Bees buzz, rain goes pitter-patter, and a balloon goes pop! These words are called onomatopoeia. Fun fact: in different languages, animals make different sounds, because people hear them differently.",
 jx:"In her poem, Lucy wrote “drip, drop, splish, splash” to sound like rain."},
{id:"g_neologism",cat:"gen",t:"Neologism",
 s:"A newly invented word or a new meaning for an existing word.",
 l:"A neologism is a new word, or an old word used in a new way. New words appear all the time, for new technology, new habits or simply for fun, such as selfie, to google or emoji. Some disappear quickly, while others become so common that dictionaries add them. Neologisms are often created by joining words, borrowing from other languages, shortening words or adding familiar endings.",
 x:"The word “staycation” started as a neologism and is now in most dictionaries.",
 f:"The word robot was a neologism: it first appeared in Karel Čapek's play R.U.R. in nineteen twenty, and Čapek said his brother Josef suggested it.",
 js:"A brand-new word that people have just started using.",
 jl:"Languages keep growing. When people invent something new, they often need a new word for it. Selfie and emoji were once brand-new words. These new words are called neologisms. Maybe you will invent one someday!",
 jx:"Ollie invented the neologism “snowcano” for the giant snow pile in the playground."},
{id:"g_pun",cat:"gen",t:"Pun",
 s:"A joke that plays on a word with two meanings or on words that sound alike.",
 l:"A pun uses the fact that one word can mean two things, or that two different words sound the same, to make a joke. For example: “I used to be a baker, but I couldn't make enough dough” plays on dough as both bread mixture and slang for money. Puns are common in headlines, adverts, Christmas cracker jokes and Shakespeare's plays. They often get a groan rather than a laugh, which many pun-lovers consider a success.",
 x:"The shop called “Hair Today, Gone Tomorrow” uses a pun on here and hair.",
 f:"Dictionaries say the origin of the word pun itself is uncertain; it appeared in English in the sixteen hundreds.",
 js:"A joke that uses a word with two meanings.",
 jl:"A pun is a joke that plays with words. For example: “Why did the scarecrow win a prize? Because he was outstanding in his field!” Outstanding means very good, but the scarecrow is also standing out in a field. Puns make people laugh or groan.",
 jx:"Dad loves puns: “I'm reading a book about anti-gravity – I can't put it down!”"},
{id:"g_synonym",cat:"gen",t:"Synonym",
 s:"A word that means the same, or nearly the same, as another word.",
 l:"Synonyms are words with the same or very similar meanings, like big and large, or begin and start. Few synonyms are perfect matches: they often differ in tone, formality or use, so a big mistake is not quite the same as a large mistake. Writers use synonyms to avoid repeating the same word and to choose the exact shade of meaning. The opposite is an antonym, a word with the opposite meaning.",
 x:"Instead of writing “nice” three times, she looked for synonyms such as kind, pleasant and friendly.",
 f:"Peter Mark Roget published his famous thesaurus, a book of words grouped by meaning, in eighteen fifty-two.",
 js:"A word that means the same as another word.",
 jl:"Happy and glad mean almost the same thing. So do big and huge, or fast and quick. Words like these are synonyms. Using different synonyms makes your stories more interesting, so you do not repeat the same word again and again.",
 jx:"Ruby found three synonyms for “said” for her story: whispered, shouted and giggled."},
{id:"g_en_queue",cat:"gen",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Queue (and queue-jumping)",
 s:"A line of people waiting their turn, and the British habit of taking it very seriously.",
 l:"In British English a queue is a line of people waiting for something, and to queue means to wait in one. Queuing in an orderly way is often seen as part of British manners, and pushing in, called queue-jumping, is considered very rude, even if people only show their annoyance with a sigh or a look. Americans usually say line and stand in line instead. Queues form at bus stops, shops, ticket offices and even outside popular bakeries.",
 x:"He tried to sneak to the front, but someone said, “Excuse me, there's a queue.”",
 f:"The word queue comes from French, where it means tail, because a line of people looks like a long tail.",
 js:"A line of people waiting their turn.",
 jl:"In Britain, when people wait for a bus or in a shop, they stand one behind another in a queue. Everyone waits their turn. Pushing to the front is called queue-jumping, and people think it is very unfair.",
 jx:"At the ice-cream van, the children made a long, neat queue."},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"p_empathy",cat:"psy",t:"Empathy",
 s:"The ability to understand and share another person's feelings.",
 l:"Empathy means sensing what someone else feels and seeing a situation from their point of view. Psychologists often distinguish cognitive empathy, understanding what someone thinks or feels, from emotional empathy, actually feeling something with them. People show empathy in different ways: some through words, others through practical help. The idea that autistic people lack empathy is a myth; differences in communication can be mistaken for a lack of caring.",
 x:"Her empathy made her a wonderful nurse; patients felt she really understood them.",
 f:"The English word empathy was coined in nineteen hundred and nine by the psychologist Edward Titchener as a translation of the German word Einfühlung, feeling into.",
 js:"Understanding how someone else feels.",
 jl:"When your friend falls over and you feel sad for them, that is empathy. It means imagining how someone else feels, as if you were in their shoes. Empathy helps us be kind and help each other.",
 jx:"Zara showed empathy when she sat with the new boy who looked lonely at lunch."},
{id:"p_intrinsic",cat:"psy",t:"Intrinsic and extrinsic motivation",
 s:"Intrinsic motivation comes from inside, from interest or enjoyment; extrinsic comes from outside, such as rewards or pressure.",
 l:"We are intrinsically motivated when we do something because it is interesting, meaningful or fun, like reading a favourite book. We are extrinsically motivated when we do it for something outside the activity: money, grades, praise or to avoid trouble. Both are normal and useful. Research suggests that people stay engaged longer when they feel some choice, a sense of progress and a connection to others. For people with ADHD, interest and urgency can be especially strong drivers.",
 x:"She played the piano for hours out of intrinsic motivation; nobody had to remind her.",
 f:"In a well-known study from the nineteen seventies, children who expected a reward for drawing later showed less interest in drawing than children who were not promised a reward.",
 js:"Doing something because you enjoy it, or because you get a reward.",
 jl:"Sometimes you do things just because you love them, like building with blocks. That is motivation from inside. Other times you do things to get a sticker or a prize. That is motivation from outside. Both can help, but doing what you love often feels best.",
 jx:"Leo practises football every day just for fun – nobody gives him stickers for it."},
{id:"p_neuroplasticity",cat:"psy",t:"Neuroplasticity",
 s:"The brain's ability to change and reorganise its connections throughout life.",
 l:"Neuroplasticity means the brain is not fixed: connections between nerve cells grow stronger when they are used often and weaker when they are not. This is how we learn skills, form habits and recover some functions after injury. Plasticity is especially high in childhood but continues into old age. It does not mean anything can be changed instantly; change usually takes repetition, time and support.",
 x:"Learning to juggle is a good example of neuroplasticity: practice slowly rewires the brain.",
 f:"A study published in two thousand found that London taxi drivers, who must learn thousands of streets, had a larger rear part of the hippocampus, a brain area involved in navigation.",
 js:"The brain's ability to change and learn new things.",
 jl:"Your brain is a bit like a path in a forest. The more you walk the same way, the clearer the path becomes. When you practise something, like riding a bike, your brain builds stronger paths. That is neuroplasticity, and it means you can always learn.",
 jx:"At first Ana found times tables hard, but with practice her brain got better at them."},
{id:"p_working_memory",cat:"psy",t:"Working memory",
 s:"The short-term mental workspace where we hold and use information for a few moments.",
 l:"Working memory lets us keep information in mind while we use it: remembering a phone number while dialling, following instructions or doing mental arithmetic. Its capacity is small, and it is easily disrupted by distractions, stress or tiredness. Working memory is one of the executive functions, and differences in it are common in ADHD, dyslexia and other neurodivergent profiles. Writing things down, breaking tasks into steps and using checklists can take the load off.",
 x:"Long verbal instructions overloaded his working memory, so his teacher wrote them on the board.",
 f:"In nineteen fifty-six the psychologist George Miller wrote about the magical number seven, plus or minus two, but later research suggests we can hold only about four chunks at once.",
 js:"The part of your mind that holds information for a short time while you use it.",
 jl:"Working memory is like a small notepad in your mind. It holds things for a little while, like the steps your teacher just said. The notepad is small, so it can get full. Writing things down helps a lot.",
 jx:"Ella wrote the three steps on her hand, so her working memory did not have to hold them all."},
{id:"p_hindsight",cat:"psy",t:"Hindsight bias",
 s:"The tendency to believe, after something has happened, that we knew it would happen.",
 l:"Hindsight bias is the feeling of “I knew it all along” after an event. Once we know an outcome, it seems more predictable than it really was, and we forget how uncertain we felt before. This can make us judge past decisions too harshly, both other people's and our own. Writing down predictions before an event is a simple way to notice the bias. Remembering that we made the best choice with the information we had can reduce unnecessary self-blame.",
 x:"After the match, everyone claimed they knew the team would lose – classic hindsight bias.",
 f:"The psychologist Baruch Fischhoff studied hindsight bias in the nineteen seventies, showing that people overestimate what they would have predicted.",
 js:"Thinking “I knew that would happen!” after it has happened.",
 jl:"After something happens, it is easy to think you knew it all along. But before, you were not really sure. That is hindsight bias. It is good to remember, so you do not feel too bad about choices you made before you knew everything.",
 jx:"“I knew it would rain!” said Tom – but this morning he left his raincoat at home."},
{id:"p_en_talking_therapies",cat:"psy",only:1,c:"England",j:false,t:"NHS Talking Therapies",
 s:"Free NHS psychological therapy services in England for common problems such as anxiety and depression.",
 l:"NHS Talking Therapies offers evidence-based psychological treatments, such as cognitive behavioural therapy and counselling, for adults in England with common problems like anxiety, depression, phobias and stress. People can usually refer themselves online or by phone, without first seeing a GP. Treatment can be one-to-one, in groups, by video or through guided self-help. The service was previously known by its programme name, IAPT, standing for Improving Access to Psychological Therapies.",
 x:"After weeks of panic attacks, she referred herself to NHS Talking Therapies.",
 f:"The programme started across England in two thousand and eight under the name Improving Access to Psychological Therapies."},
/* ---------- BUSINESS (+6) ---------- */
{id:"b_startup",cat:"biz",t:"Startup",
 s:"A young company built to grow quickly around a new product or business idea.",
 l:"A startup is a new business that tries to find a model that can grow fast, often using technology. Unlike a typical small business, which may aim to stay local and stable, a startup usually aims to scale up, and it often relies on investors who accept high risk in exchange for a share of the company. Many startups fail, while a few become very large. Typical words around startups include seed funding, pivot, MVP and scale-up.",
 x:"The startup began in a student flat and now employs fifty people.",
 f:"The term unicorn for a startup valued at over a billion dollars was coined by the investor Aileen Lee in two thousand and thirteen, because such companies seemed so rare.",
 js:"A brand-new company with a big new idea that wants to grow fast.",
 jl:"A startup is a new company that begins with a fresh idea, like a new app or a clever gadget. It often starts very small, maybe in a garage or a kitchen. The people hope it will grow big quickly, but it is a risky adventure.",
 jx:"Maya's aunt runs a startup that makes reusable lunch wraps."},
{id:"b_supply_chain",cat:"biz",t:"Supply chain",
 s:"The whole network of steps and companies that bring a product from raw materials to the customer.",
 l:"A supply chain includes everything needed to get a product to you: raw materials, factories, transport, warehouses, shops and delivery. A simple T-shirt may involve cotton farmers, spinning and dyeing companies, garment factories and shipping firms in several countries. When one link breaks, for example because of a storm, a strike or a shortage, delays can spread along the whole chain. Companies try to make their supply chains efficient, reliable and, increasingly, ethical and lower in emissions.",
 x:"Problems in the supply chain meant the shop ran out of bicycles for months.",
 f:"In March twenty twenty-one the giant container ship Ever Given got stuck in the Suez Canal for six days, holding up hundreds of ships and goods worldwide.",
 js:"All the steps that get something from where it is made to the shop.",
 jl:"Your cereal does not just appear in the shop. Farmers grow the grain, a factory makes the flakes, lorries carry the boxes, and the shop puts them on shelves. All these steps together are the supply chain. If one step stops, the shelves can be empty.",
 jx:"The class drew the supply chain of a chocolate bar, from cocoa farm to corner shop."},
{id:"b_crowdfunding",cat:"biz",t:"Crowdfunding",
 s:"Raising money for a project from many people, each giving a small amount, usually online.",
 l:"Crowdfunding collects money from a large number of supporters instead of one bank or investor. On reward-based platforms, people get a product or a thank-you gift; in donation crowdfunding they simply support a cause; in equity crowdfunding they get a small share of a company. It lets creators test whether there is demand for an idea. However, projects can be delayed or fail, so backers should understand that it is not the same as buying from a shop.",
 x:"The band used crowdfunding to pay for recording their first album.",
 f:"In eighteen eighty-five the newspaper publisher Joseph Pulitzer raised money for the Statue of Liberty's pedestal from well over a hundred thousand donors, many giving less than a dollar.",
 js:"Lots of people each giving a little money to help a project happen.",
 jl:"Imagine your class wants a new bench for the playground. If everyone gives a few coins, you can buy it together. That is the idea of crowdfunding: many people each give a little, usually on a website, so a project can come true.",
 jx:"The school used crowdfunding to build a new nature garden."},
{id:"b_benchmarking",cat:"biz",t:"Benchmarking",
 s:"Comparing your performance or processes with the best in your field to find ways to improve.",
 l:"Benchmarking means measuring your results, such as costs, speed, quality or customer satisfaction, against others, usually industry leaders or the best departments in your own organisation. The aim is not to copy blindly but to learn what makes others successful and set realistic targets. It can compare products, processes or whole strategies. In computing, a benchmark also means a standard test used to compare the speed of devices.",
 x:"Benchmarking showed that competitors delivered orders in two days, while they took five.",
 f:"Originally a benchmark was a mark cut into stone by surveyors, used as a fixed reference point when measuring heights.",
 js:"Comparing yourself with the best to see how to get better.",
 jl:"If you want to run faster, you might watch the fastest runner in your class and see what they do differently. Companies do the same thing. They compare themselves with the best and learn from them. That is benchmarking.",
 jx:"Our school did some benchmarking and visited another school with a brilliant library."},
{id:"b_brand",cat:"biz",t:"Brand",
 s:"The name, look and reputation that make a company or product recognisable and distinct.",
 l:"A brand is more than a logo: it is the whole impression people have of a company or product, built from its name, design, colours, voice, quality and customer experiences. A strong brand helps customers recognise and trust a product and can let a company charge more. Branding is the work of shaping that impression. A brand can be damaged quickly by poor service or scandal, and rebuilding trust takes a long time.",
 x:"The café built its brand around friendly service and locally roasted coffee.",
 f:"The word brand originally meant fire or a burning piece of wood, and later the mark burned onto cattle to show who owned them.",
 js:"The name and look that help you recognise a company or product.",
 jl:"When you see a certain logo or colour and know straight away which company it is, that is because of its brand. A brand is the name, the look and what people think about it. Companies work hard to make their brand friendly and easy to remember.",
 jx:"Ben could recognise his favourite cereal brand from across the shop."},
{id:"b_en_companies_house",cat:"biz",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Companies House",
 s:"The UK government agency that registers companies and keeps public information about them.",
 l:"Companies House is where limited companies in the UK are formed and registered. Every company must send it certain information, such as its registered office address, directors, annual accounts and a yearly confirmation statement. Much of this information is public and can be searched online for free, which helps customers, suppliers and journalists check who they are dealing with. Recent reforms have given it more powers to check identities and tackle false information.",
 x:"Before signing the contract, she checked the supplier's details on Companies House.",
 f:"Companies House has its headquarters in Cardiff, the capital of Wales.",
 js:"A UK office where all companies are written down in a big public list.",
 jl:"In the UK, when people start a company, they must register it at Companies House. It keeps a big list of companies and who runs them. Anyone can look at the list online, which helps people check that a company is real.",
 jx:"Mum looked up the toy company on Companies House to see when it was started."}
]);
